=20
> "Le tolvirnu cinfo" is, of course, not a name; and it is well to remember=
=20
> that "le" may be glossed "a(n)" just as well as "the", so it's as if CL=
=20
> were constantly referred to as "a cowardly lion", which is something nobo=
dy=20
> but Jaqen H'ghar would do. "le" primarily encodes specificity whereas=20
> English names (not even those of "the X" sort) don't.=20
>
> It's interesting to wonder what translation would have been suitable had=
=20
> the original not been a name, and if instead the character were simply=20
> always referred to as "the cowardly lion": the main semantic ingredient=
=20
> contributed by the "the" then would be the definiteness. "lo bi'u nai=20
> tolvirnu cinfo" captures the definiteness but of course foregrounds it to=
o=20
> much, and probably "lo tolvirnu cinfo" would be the best rendition.
>
> What motivated me to make my original response was not just that "le=20
> tolvirnu cinfo" is very inferior to "la tolvirnu cinfo" as a rendition of=
=20
> the English but also that it was a change inflicted on somebody else's=20
> translation and furthermore was inflicted on it as if it were a correctio=
n=20
> rather than an incorrection.
